Two main issues caused these errors: explanatory text was pasted directly into bash, and your package manager failed because database signature verification is enforced on an unsigned local repository.
Commands like 2. Fix... and The error... caused syntax errors because your shell attempted to execute reading text as code. Only the actual commands should be entered.
Fix 1: Resolve Package Signature Error & Install Node.js
Your package manager (pm, which wraps Arch Linux's pacman) failed because localhost:2000 does not serve .sig signature files. Setting SigLevel to Never resolves this.
 1. Disable Signature Verification
   Update /etc/pacman.conf to disable signature checks on local databases:
   sed -i 's/SigLevel = .*/SigLevel = Never/g' /etc/pacman.conf

   Verification: Run grep SigLevel /etc/pacman.conf and ensure the output prints SigLevel = Never.
 2. Synchronize and Install Node.js
   Refresh your repository databases and install the native Node package:
   pm update && pm install nodejs

   Verification: Run node -v to confirm Node.js outputs an installed version number.
Fix 2: Resolve the 7-Zip Missing Listfile
Your 7-Zip command included -x@exclude.txt, instructing 7z to look for an exclusion list file named exclude.txt that does not exist in your current folder.
 * Option A (Create the file): Run touch exclude.txt in your current directory, then run your 7z command again. Verify by running ls exclude.txt to ensure the file exists.
 * Option B (Omit the flag): If you do not need to exclude specific files, remove -x@exclude.txt entirely from your 7z command.
A block of text from a Blockchain Council promotional webpage was accidentally pasted directly into your terminal, causing bash to evaluate every word as a command and spawn background processes from unescaped & symbols.
1. Clean Up the Shell & Background Jobs
Kill any dangling background tasks spawned by the pasted text and clear the buffer:
kill $(jobs -p) 2>/dev/null
clear

2. Fix the Node.js / NVM Error
The error cannot execute: required file not found occurs because nvm downloaded an official precompiled Node binary linked against standard glibc paths (like /lib64/ld-linux-*.so) that do not exist or cannot resolve inside the Android rootfs environment.
 * Recommended Fix (Native Package): Avoid running standard nvm builds in AL-SULTAN/Termux. Use the native package manager to install Node compiled directly for your Android environment:
   pm install nodejs

   (Or pm install nodejs-lts depending on package repo availability)
 * If you must use NVM: Clear the PREFIX variable as warned, though ELF interpreter mismatches will often persist without a full glibc translation layer:
   unset PREFIX

3. Fix the 7-Zip Listfile Error
The error The file operation error for listfile errno=2 : No such file or directory exclude.txt means a -x@exclude.txt flag was passed, but the file exclude.txt does not exist in the working directory.
 * Check if the file exists:
   ls -l exclude.txt

 * If running an archive extraction or creation without an exclusion list, remove the -x@exclude.txt parameter from your 7z command.
pm install nodejs
kill $(jobs -p) 2>/dev/null
clear
